p with his  plan they're gonna go rook e8 to allow  Knight to f8 Dwight continues with  Knight f1 and here black notices white  is trying to go to g3 and he tries to  think how can we stop White's pieces  from being so active if white moves his  knight to g3 it's looking at coming to  f5 and h5 it can't go to h5 right away  but watch I'm limit these squares for  the white tonight so black is gonna try  and prepare g6 and make his pieces  revolver on that if his Knight goes to  f8 it won't be able to go to g6 because  the pawn will be there slowly moves  Bishop to f8 after g6 he's gonna try and  put the bishop on g7 here white  continues Knight g3 and then g6  restricting the night White has  completed his plan on the King side his  two very well placed Knights both of his  bishops are facing this direction so  he's gonna try and gain expansion on the  Queen side try and get some advantage  over there so he's gonna go a 4 and  black counter strikes in the center with  c5 and white wants to shut this down  shut this idea down keep a space  advantage and trying to convert to a win  so you're gonna go d5 and then black  trying to keep his Center solid is gonna  place c5 and this is the Brier opening  both sides are going to be trying to  slowly develop their pieces and it's all  going to be a fight over the center the  most common continuation will be Bishop  to g5 h6 and Bishop to e3 all of whites  pieces have a purpose all of blacks  pieces also have a purpose  because it's not the best opening for  black it doesn't have a very good score  but hopefully this gives you guys some  idea of how the pieces develop and work  together so that is it for the rule  apes' or prior defense  [Music]

